DF(1)                          P32W Unix                           DF(1)

NAME
       df - free space on the mounted filesystems

SYNOPSIS
       df [special ...]

DESCRIPTION
       df reports the size, the space used and the space free of
       the root filesystem (/dev/disk, mounted on /) and of each
       filesystem listed in /etc/mtab, in 1024-byte blocks, with
       the percentage used. With special arguments (block devices
       such as /dev/ram) it reports just those, each with the
       directory /etc/mtab says it is mounted on.

       The counts of a mounted filesystem come from the kernel
       (ustat(2)), so any user may run df and the numbers are
       current. A device that is not mounted is read from its
       superblock on the device, which only root can do.

       Used counts every block not free, including the filesystem's
       own superblock, inode and bitmap blocks.

EXAMPLES
       # df
       Filesystem   1K-blocks    Used   Avail Use%  Mounted on
       /dev/disk        15360    3483   11877  22%  /
       /dev/ram           256       7     249   2%  /mnt

FILES
       /etc/mtab      the mounted filesystems (written by mount(8))
       /dev/disk      the root filesystem

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 if some filesystem could not be read (its line says
       cannot read its superblock).

SEE ALSO
       du(1), ustat(2), mount(8), mkfs(8), fsck(8), mtab(5), fs(5)

BUGS
       There are no options; a file or directory argument is not
       turned into its filesystem, and inodes are not reported.
       The root device is always taken to be /dev/disk.
